# EXAMPLE: crontab entry that keeps one container site up to date every hour:
# first `git pull` of the h5adstore (the dataset .md / .h5ad.mus / index.md
# metadata, which the ingest watcher commits and pushes), then
# `ant-stack ingest -- ant sync pull` (datasets, the paper library PDFs,
# _facet_tests/). The pull asks the api to rescan, so new datasets appear
# without a restart. An hour with nothing new costs one S3 listing; flock
# keeps two runs from overlapping when a large new dataset takes longer. Install with `crontab -e` as the user that
# owns the stores (the run_as uid of site.yaml) and may run docker. Fill the
# <PLACEHOLDERS>.
#
# cron starts jobs with PATH=/usr/bin:/bin. ant-stack and ant-sync begin with
# `#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run ...`, so uv must be on PATH: the installer puts it
# in ~/.local/bin, which cron does not search. Without the PATH line below the
# job dies with "env: 'uv': No such file or directory" (nothing from ant-stack
# itself, it never got to start). `ant-stack check` prints where uv is.
# Alternative: call uv explicitly, `<HOME>/.local/bin/uv run --script
# <REPO_PATH>/bin/ops/ant-stack ...`.

PATH=<HOME>/.local/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
ANTHIVE_SITE=<REPO_PATH>/site.yaml

17 * * * *  flock -n /tmp/anthive-sync.lock sh -c 'git -C <H5ADSTORE_PATH> pull --ff-only -q; <REPO_PATH>/bin/ops/ant-stack ingest -- ant sync pull' >> <LOG_DIR>/sync-pull.log 2>&1
